---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";

const installCode = `pnpm add composite-js`;

const createCode = `import { chart, stackY } from "composite-js";

const totals = [
  { category: "A", value: 12 },
  { category: "B", value: 18 },
];

const trend = [
  { year: 2023, value: 8 },
  { year: 2024, value: 14 },
  { year: 2025, value: 17 },
];

const bars = chart({
  mark: "bar",
  data: totals,
  encoding: { x: "category", y: "value" },
});

const line = chart({
  mark: "line",
  data: trend,
  encoding: { x: "year", y: "value" },
});

const view = stackY([bars, line]);`;

const renderCode = `<div id="app"></div>

<script type="module">
  view.render(document.querySelector("#app"));
</script>`;
---

<DocsLayout title="Getting Started | composite.js">
  <div class="docs-content">
    <header class="docs-hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">Getting Started</p>
      <h1>Create and render your first composite view</h1>
      <p>
        Define the charts, express how they relate, and render the result.
        composite.js supplies the chart implementations and computes the
        concrete layout.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="docs-section">
      <h2>Install</h2>
      <pre class="docs-code"><code>{installCode}</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
      <h2>Define charts and their relationship</h2>
      <p>
        Each chart leaf uses a mark, rows of data, and an encoding object. The
        encoding keys are visual channels; the values are field names from your
        rows. <code>stackY([bars, line])</code> expresses only that the two
        charts form a vertical sequence; it does not require coordinates or
        manual size calculations.
      </p>
      <pre class="docs-code"><code>{createCode}</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
      <h2>Start with defaults</h2>
      <p>
        The example above does not specify chart sizes, stack spacing, or
        alignment targets. Built-in defaults and content measurement provide a
        complete layout. Add <code>width</code>, <code>height</code>,
        <code>margin</code>, or <code>align</code> only when the design calls for
        a specific result.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
      <h2>Render</h2>
      <p>
        Every layout node exposes <code>render()</code>. When you render a
        composed node, composite.js measures, positions, and draws the whole
        tree.
      </p>
      <pre class="docs-code"><code>{renderCode}</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
      <h2>Next steps</h2>
      <dl class="api-list">
        <div>
          <dt>Learn the model</dt>
          <dd>Read Concepts for abstract relationships, automatic layout, and recursive composition.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Check signatures</dt>
          <dd>Use the API page when you need function names, options, or render/export behavior.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Choose marks</dt>
          <dd>Use the Marks page to match each chart type with its required encoding channels.</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </section>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
